## 5. Detailed decisions and failure behaviour

### Fanout, mode changes and bounded retrieval

Ordinary-author workers page through active follower IDs, idempotently upsert references and checkpoint progress. A crash after a batch commit can repeat the batch. Isolate work by author/priority so a busy account cannot monopolize the queue. An outbox preserves the post-to-fanout intent; it does not make every follower update part of the post transaction.

For expensive authors, use the recent author index at read time. Bound the candidate plan: 200 ordinary references, up to 50 pull authors with 10 posts each, selected by favorite first, then newest currently eligible post, then author ID, and 20 own posts. This gives at most 720 initial IDs before dedup/filter/ranking and the 200-ID session cap. Author activity selection and coverage metrics are part of that design. An account following thousands of high-fanout authors needs budgeted retrieval, not an unbounded query for every followee.

When an author changes mode, overlap push and pull briefly under a mode version/source-time boundary. Prepare the pull index before stopping push. Deduplication handles overlap; it cannot repair a gap where neither path supplies the post. Mode changes affect delivery of references, never privacy ownership.

Skipping inactive followers saves writes only if returning readers have a bounded rebuild from author indexes and then rejoin live updates. Rate-limit rebuilds during recovery. Merge the viewer's own recent committed posts directly so their immediate read does not depend on follower fanout completing.

### Ranking and cursor semantics

V1 uses `score = createdAtMillis + (favorite ? 30 minutes : 0)`, then `(score DESC, createdAt DESC, postId ASC)`. A favorite post competes as if it were thirty minutes newer. Favorite changes affect new sessions; the current session keeps its order. Creation time and score inputs are server-owned, so a caller cannot submit a future timestamp or arbitrary rank.

A learned ranker can later add affinity/engagement/content features. It needs versioned models, feature freshness, timeouts, deterministic fallback and evaluation. It still ranks a retrieved, authorized candidate set; it does not solve missing candidates, deletion or pagination.

The cursor authenticates viewer, session and next candidate index. Page size and ranker version are fixed in the session. Use HMAC/authenticated tokens or an equally protected server handle; base64 alone is encoding. A five-minute expiry or capacity eviction returns `410` with refresh required. Do not silently start a different ordering under an old cursor.

Restart must not bind an old token to a new session with a reused counter. This local core includes its instance incarnation in the MAC. A distributed service needs shared session state/identities or explicit owner routing; independent local maps behind a random load balancer do not provide continuation availability.

For a purely chronological feed, indexed keyset pagination with a unique immutable `(createdAt,postId)` boundary can be enough. Its cost depends on the index and scanned rows, not a universal O(1) claim. [PostgreSQL 17 LIMIT/OFFSET guidance](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/queries-limit.html) explains unique ordering and why large offsets still require skipped rows to be computed. A score cursor by itself does not stabilize a ranked set whose scores change.

### Current authorization is a separate authority operation

Each item needs a non-deleted canonical post, the current home-feed relationship/audience and no block in either direction. Authors can read their own non-deleted author-only posts. A public-post permission does not override our home-feed following requirement. An inbox/session membership is never a permanent allowed flag.

Initially, one database transaction can read post/graph policy facts coherently. Splitting them across shards/services requires preserving a defined current authorization gate with consistent reads/policy revisions. If current permission cannot be established, omit the affected item or fail closed. An arbitrarily stale replica or five-minute permission cache cannot support the chosen semantics. Batch policy calls and measure their throughput/latency; a cached body may be reusable only after its current identity/version and permission have been checked. [OWASP authorization guidance](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html) covers checks on every request and protected static resources.

A revocation committed before the next valid gate affects that gate. A change after the decision cannot recall bytes already serialized/sent. Private media needs protected downloads; short-lived signed URLs may remain usable until expiry unless the edge checks current policy. Cache purge and a feed tombstone cannot erase previously downloaded content or screenshots. State that boundary rather than promising instant global deletion.

### Complete Java 21 core

`FeedEngine` combines fixture authority records, bounded reference inboxes and bounded ranking sessions. It demonstrates hybrid deduplication, frozen ranking, authenticated viewer-bound cursors and fresh page filtering. Helpers model follow/block/privacy/deletion changes; they are not a production post API or graph adapter.

Session creation comes from the runnable source:

```java
public synchronized Page open(String viewer,int pageSize) {
    text(viewer);if(pageSize<1||pageSize>20)throw new Problem(400,"page size must be 1..20");
    Set<String> ids=new LinkedHashSet<>();
    for(Ref ref:inboxes.getOrDefault(viewer,new java.util.TreeSet<>(NEWEST)))ids.add(ref.id());
    // This toy scans maps to derive activity; production uses a viewer activity index.
    Map<String,Long> recent=new HashMap<>();
    for(Post post:posts.values())if(pullAuthors.contains(post.author())
            &&follows.contains(new Edge(viewer,post.author()))&&eligible(viewer,post))
        recent.merge(post.author(),post.createdMillis(),Math::max);
    List<String> selected=recent.keySet().stream()
            .sorted(Comparator.<String>comparingInt(author->favorites.contains(new Edge(viewer,author))?0:1)
                .thenComparing(Comparator.<String>comparingLong(recent::get).reversed()).thenComparing(author->author))
            .limit(50).toList();
    for(String author:selected)
        posts.values().stream().filter(p->p.author().equals(author)).sorted(Comparator.comparingLong(Post::createdMillis).reversed().thenComparing(Post::id))
                .limit(10).forEach(p->ids.add(p.id()));
    posts.values().stream().filter(p->p.author().equals(viewer)).sorted(Comparator.comparingLong(Post::createdMillis).reversed().thenComparing(Post::id))
            .limit(20).forEach(p->ids.add(p.id()));
    if(ids.size()>720)throw new IllegalStateException("candidate plan exceeded 200 + 50*10 + 20");
    List<Ranked> ranked=new ArrayList<>();
    for(String id:ids){Post p=posts.get(id);if(eligible(viewer,p)){
        long boost=favorites.contains(new Edge(viewer,p.author()))?Duration.ofMinutes(30).toMillis():0;
        ranked.add(new Ranked(id,Math.addExact(p.createdMillis(),boost),p.createdMillis()));
    }}
    List<String> frozen=ranked.stream().sorted(RANK).limit(maxCandidates).map(Ranked::id).toList();
    long born=ticker.getAsLong();sessions.entrySet().removeIf(entry->expired(entry.getValue(),born));
    if(sessions.size()==maxSessions)sessions.remove(sessions.keySet().iterator().next());
    long id=Math.incrementExact(nextSession);nextSession=id;
    sessions.put(id,new Session(viewer,frozen,pageSize,born,ids.size(),selected.size()));
    return page(viewer,cursor(viewer,id,0));
}
```

Continuation advances over **scanned** IDs and checks current local authority:

```java
public synchronized Page page(String viewer,String cursor) {
    text(viewer);Position position=decode(viewer,cursor);Session session=sessions.get(position.session());
    if(session==null||expired(session,ticker.getAsLong())){
        sessions.remove(position.session());throw new Problem(410,"feed session expired or evicted; refresh");
    }
    if(!session.viewer().equals(viewer))throw new Problem(403,"cursor belongs to another viewer");
    int index=position.index();
    if(index<0||index>session.ids().size())throw new Problem(400,"invalid cursor position");
    int scanned=0;List<Item> items=new ArrayList<>();
    while(index<session.ids().size()&&items.size()<session.pageSize()&&scanned<scanBudget){
        Post post=posts.get(session.ids().get(index++));scanned++;
        if(eligible(viewer,post))items.add(new Item(post.id(),post.author(),post.body(),post.version()));
    }
    String next=index<session.ids().size()?cursor(viewer,position.session(),index):null;
    return new Page(position.session(),List.copyOf(items),next,scanned,index);
}

private boolean eligible(String viewer,Post post) {
    if(post==null||post.deleted())return false;
    if(viewer.equals(post.author()))return true;
    Edge edge=new Edge(viewer,post.author());
    if(!follows.contains(edge)||blocks.contains(edge)||blocks.contains(new Edge(post.author(),viewer)))return false;
    return post.visibility()==Visibility.PUBLIC||post.visibility()==Visibility.FOLLOWERS;
}
```

The full source supplies immutable returns, cursor verification, instance binding, exact duration expiry and capacity eviction. Its toy author scan stands for the bounded batched index reads described above, not a scalable database query. The checks use a public test key; production needs managed secret keys. A duration ticker measures session lifetime independently of source post creation timestamps.

