### Failures, security and observations

Fanout lag can yield an incomplete feed. Expose freshness or use a capped recent-author fallback; do not scan every followee per request during an outage. Cold inbox rebuilds need origin concurrency/rate limits. Ranking failure can use the declared fallback for a new session, then freeze it. Lost session state requires refresh. Unavailable policy authority must not silently broaden access.

Partition inbox/session state by viewer with virtual buckets and controlled migration. Replication of disposable references may lose recent coverage or sessions on failover; it must never become authority for a privacy decision. Durable post/request/outbox recovery has a different contract. Rebuild derived data in bounded, idempotent batches from canonical records/events.

Authenticate publishers, enforce edit/delete ownership, rate-limit posting/follow churn and bound text/media sizes. Escape rendered text, constrain media URLs and apply moderation/reporting policies. Keep hidden IDs/bodies out of customer diagnostics/logs. This walkthrough's server diagnostics use synthetic fixtures only. Minimize retention of session/ranking signals and protect session ownership and tenant boundaries.

Measure response latency/errors, fanout age, author-index lag, retrieval coverage, candidate-to-visible ratio, empty continuing pages, policy-check latency/errors, session evictions/expiry and per-author amplification. Use sampled audits to detect stale visibility regressions. IDs belong in access-controlled traces, not unbounded metric labels.

Failure/load exercises include celebrity bursts, unfollow during fanout, private/delete after session creation, blocks between pages, delayed source events, cold caches, policy outage, ranker timeout and new posts mid-session. Report omitted/error responses alongside p99. The proposed five-second/200 ms targets require separate distributed evidence.

## 6. Interviewer follow-ups

**Why not push everything?** A 20-million-follower post can consume the fleet and waste work for inactive readers. Hybrid retrieval trades that tail for bounded read-time work and an explicit coverage cost.

**Why not pull every author?** It avoids inbox writes but repeatedly merges many timelines. Bound retrieval and rebuilds; otherwise outage fallback becomes a source overload.

**Must deletion synchronously edit every inbox?** No. Commit the tombstone, filter at the next current gate and clean references later. Already delivered content cannot be recalled.

**Does a frozen session keep old privacy?** No. Only IDs/order are frozen. Permission and current content identity are checked again.

**Can an empty page have a cursor?** Yes, when its scan budget was spent on hidden candidates. Null continuation marks exhaustion.

**Are duplicates impossible?** A forward traversal of the deduplicated frozen sequence does not repeat IDs. A retried request can repeat its response, and refresh can intentionally show an old post again.

**Why not hold a database snapshot for five minutes?** It retains resources and old policy. Store bounded IDs/order and reauthorize instead.

**What ships first?** Durable posts/graph, ordinary idempotent fanout, bounded hybrid retrieval, deterministic ranking, current visibility and explicit pagination. Add learned ranking only after measuring safety, coverage and latency.

## 7. Closing answer

I would keep posts/visibility authoritative, materialize ordinary-author references and pull expensive-author candidates. A bounded session freezes a deduplicated order, while every page checks current follow, block, privacy and deletion rules. Its cursor advances over scanned IDs, including hidden ones, and expires explicitly. Hybrid fanout and bounded rebuilds control scale without turning a cached reference into permission or promising to recall content already sent.
